      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by expressing both sides of the equation with the same base. However, since 7 and 8 are not powers of each other, we will use logarithms to solve for \\(x\\).\n\nFirst, take the natural logarithm (or any logarithm, but we will use the natural logarithm for convenience) of both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nNext, distribute the logarithms:\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(x\\), move all terms involving \\(x\\) to one side of the equation:\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 - x \\ln 8 = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the left side:\n\\[\nx (\\ln 7 - \\ln 8) = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(x\\) by dividing both sides by \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\n\nWe can further simplify the right side of the equation. Notice that \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8 = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\\), so the equation becomes:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nRewrite the logarithm expression in a more familiar form:\n\\[\nx = \\log_{\\frac{7}{8}} 7^7\n\\]\n\nThus, the base \\(b\\) is \\(\\frac{7}{8}\\), and the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{8}}\n\\]",
