      "text": "We are given the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\). To solve for \\(x\\), we start by expressing both sides of the equation with the same base. However, since 7 and 8 are not perfect powers of each other, we will take the common logarithm (base 10) of both sides to simplify the equation.\n\nStarting with the equation:\n\\[7^{x+7} = 8^x\\]\n\nTaking the common logarithm (base 10) of both sides, we get:\n\\[\\log(7^{x+7}) = \\log(8^x)\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule \\(\\log(a^b) = b \\log(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\\[(x + 7) \\log 7 = x \\log 8\\]\n\nNext, we distribute \\(\\log 7\\) on the left side:\n\\[x \\log 7 + 7 \\log 7 = x \\log 8\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(x\\), we move all terms involving \\(x\\) to one side of the equation:\n\\[x \\log 7 - x \\log 8 = -7 \\log 7\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the left side:\n\\[x (\\log 7 - \\log 8) = -7 \\log 7\\]\n\nSolve for \\(x\\) by dividing both sides by \\(\\log 7 - \\log 8\\):\n\\[x = \\frac{-7 \\log 7}{\\log 7 - \\log 8}\\]\n\nWe are given that the solution can be expressed in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). Since \\(7^7 = 8^x\\) and we have already solved for \\(x\\), we can see that:\n\\[x = \\log_b 7^7\\]\n\nBy comparing the expressions for \\(x\\), we see that:\n\\[x = \\frac{-7 \\log 7}{\\log 7 - \\log 8}\\]\n\nMultiplying the numerator and the denominator by \\(-1\\), we get:\n\\[x = \\frac{7 \\log 7}{\\log 8 - \\log 7}\\]\n\nSince this matches the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\), we identify that \\(b\\) must be the base of the logarithm on the right side of the equation. Therefore, we have:\n\\[b = 8\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(b\\) is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
